What is @EntityGraph?

@EntityGraph is a JPA annotation that tells Hibernate to fetch related entities in the same SQL query for a specific repository method.

It helps solve one of the most common performance problems in JPA called the N+1 Query Problem.

Example Without @EntityGraph

Suppose your Product entity has:

@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "category_id")
private Category category;

You execute:

List<Product> products = productRepository.findAll();

Hibernate runs:

SELECT * FROM products;

Now you loop through the products:

for (Product product : products) {
    System.out.println(product.getCategory().getName());
}

Hibernate executes one extra query for each product:

SELECT * FROM categories WHERE id = 1;
SELECT * FROM categories WHERE id = 2;
SELECT * FROM categories WHERE id = 3;
...

If you have 100 products:

1 query for products
100 queries for categories

Total = 101 queries

This is called the N+1 Query Problem.

Using @EntityGraph

Repository:

@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "category")
Page<Product> findByDeletedFalse(Pageable pageable);

Now Hibernate generates a single query like:

SELECT p.*, c.*
FROM products p
LEFT JOIN categories c
ON p.category_id = c.id;

Now:

1 query for products
0 extra queries for categories

Total = 1 query

Why Not Use FetchType.EAGER?

Many beginners think:

@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)

is the solution.

It isn't.

Suppose Category later has:

Parent Category
Products
Attributes

Every time you load a Product, Hibernate may load much more data than you actually need.

That's why enterprise applications follow this rule:

Default = FetchType.LAZY
Load relationships only when needed using @EntityGraph or JOIN FETCH
When Should You Use @EntityGraph?

Use it when you know you'll need related data.

Examples:

@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "category")
Optional<Product> findById(Long id);

or

@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"category", "images"})
Page<Product> findByDeletedFalse(Pageable pageable);

@EntityGraph vs JOIN FETCH

@EntityGraph

Simple to use

Works with Spring Data repository methods
Cleaner code
Preferred for common use cases

JOIN FETCH

@Query("""
SELECT p
FROM Product p
JOIN FETCH p.category
WHERE p.id = :id
""")
More flexible
Better for complex JPQL queries
Enterprise Best Practice

In professional Spring Boot projects:

Keep all relationships LAZY.
Use @EntityGraph when a repository method always needs related entities.
Use JOIN FETCH for complex custom queries.
Avoid making relationships EAGER by default.


